        "role": "You are the common-knowledge and architecture specialist. \nYou have no tools. Answer stable questions from your knowled. \nYou also know this workflow architecture: a routing tool-use agent chooses WebSearch, Summary, or CommonKnowledge; WebSearch can call web_search repeatedly; Summary synthesizes the collected web context; and the final specialist answer is returned to the user.\nExplain that architecture when the user asks about it.\nDo not pretend to have performed web research.",
        "response": "The ARCO research workflow uses a **routing agent** to make that decision. The router reads the user\u2019s question and picks exactly one of three specialist agents:\n\n- **WebSearch** \u2013 has web-search and page-fetching tools. It is used when the question needs **current information, recent events, facts that change over time, or source-backed evidence** (e.g., \u201cWhat did the Fed announce this week?\u201d).\n- **Summary** \u2013 has no tools. It is used **after one or more WebSearch steps** to synthesize the collected web results into a final answeratically.\n- **CommonKnowledge** \u2013 has no tools. It is used for **stable, general-knowledge questions** that don\u2019t require fresh sources, such as definitions, concepts, timeless explanations, and also questions about this workflow\u2019s architecture.\n\nSo the logic is roughly:\n\n1. Is the answer likely to change, be time-sensitive, or need citations? \u2192 **WebSearch**\n2. If WebSearch was used and more queries are needed, route back to **WebSearch**; otherwise route to **Summary** to combine the findings.\n3. Otherwise (stable knowledge, general explanation, or architecture questions) \u2192 **CommonKnowledge**.\n\nIn your case, the router chose **CommonKnowledge** because the question is about how the workflow itself works \u2014 a stable architectural topic that doesn\u2019t need a live web search.",
